    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d548">

                <pb facs="0634" n="532" xml:id="pg532"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12466: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-09-20T12:00:00+08:00">September 20,
                        1933—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received September 20—6:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>756. Following from the American Consulate at Foochow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 18, 9 a.m. My September 5, noon. The situation at Yenping has
                            reached an apparent stalemate with the Fifty-sixth Nationalist Division
                            still holding that city; the Communists a short distance to the north of
                            Yenping; and the Nineteenth Route Army to the south. The presence of a
                            United States naval vessel at Foochow is believed to be no longer
                            essential.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>2. I have repeated message to Admiral Upham<anchor n="52" xml:id="d548-f1"/> who
                    is anxious to remove American war vessel elsewhere.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d548-f1" n="52" xml:id="d548-f1-content">
                <p>Admiral Frank B. Upham, commander in chief, U. S. Asiatic Fleet.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
